@@ -1,5 +1,11 @@
 ## Unreleased

+ - Added `read: true` to `fetch_url` and to the tools of an MCP server, which asks for the result in the result of the call, as `run` has it. Where permissions are bypassed with no screening, or auto-vetting is on and the check says `safe`, the page or the reply comes back at once with the label and audit entry a `vet_content` call would have given it, which saves a model round. Any other answer from the check, a check that cannot be made, a session that asks you, a picture, content past the output cap and a session not offered `vet_content` keep the reference, and nothing of the check's word or reason is shown. A server that lists its own argument named `read` keeps it. ([#3722](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3722))
+ - Fixed a delegate started after its parent had been shown private content being told it could hand quarantined files to processors, being offered `spawn_processor`, and having every such call refused with text that said "this conversation" and pointed at `vet_content`, which a delegate is never offered. Such a delegate now starts without `spawn_processor`, is told that it was started holding the private content of the context that asked it and should say in its answer that it could not have a processor look at the content, and a refusal names only the routes the run is offered. The `spawn_agent` description says that a task needing a picture or file looked at is started before then or left to you, the `read_output` description says that being shown output closes processors for delegates started afterwards, and a `reader` is described as running processors only while the context that started it holds nothing private. ([#3725](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3725))
+ - Fixed the agent telling itself that nothing will show it a quarantined file, not even asking. It is now told that another read or a search will not show it, and a turn offered `vet_content` is sent to it first. A one-shot run is told to say in its reply how you can lift the quarantine on another run (`--trust-workspace`, `--file`, and `--vet` where it applies) and not to end on a question nobody can answer. ([#3723](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3723))
+ - Changed the `each` field of `spawn_agent` to say that one call starts at most eight delegates and that a further call with the same `task` starts more, so a fan-out of more than eight is split the first time and not after a refused call. The refusal is unchanged. ([#3726](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3726))
+ - Added a warning to a delegate with a token budget. It is sent once, in the request after the round at which the delegate has spent four fifths of the budget or has less left than two requests at its current context size cost, and asks it to finish or undo what is open, commit or discard its edits, and answer with what it did and what is left, before it loses its tools. The prompt of a delegate with a budget says its tokens are bounded, and the `budget` field of `spawn_agent` says every request counts the whole conversation, so a figure buys a few dozen requests. ([#3724](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3724))
+ - Fixed the first turn checking each MCP server's tool list one after another. The checks of every list to be put are now made at the same time, at most eight at once, so the wait before the first request is about one check's for up to eight servers, and the questions are still drawn in the order the servers are declared. A turn that spends a Premium subscription still makes them one after another. ([#3721](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3721))
  - Added the questions of an `ask_user` call and what became of each under the call in the terminal, in a session in lines and in the desktop window: the option you picked, the words you typed, `skipped` or `closed on the clock`. The line `3 questions asked` stays. The rows are on your screen only and are not given to the agent or kept in the session. ([#3712](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3712))
  - Fixed the list of commands under the box that opens when you type `/` stopping at the last row the window had room for, so that Down moved onto commands that were never drawn. The list now scrolls to keep the command under the cursor in view, and Down, the wheel and a click reach every command and file name in it.
  - Added `bravebot schedule pause`, `resume`, `pending` and `clear`. A paused entry keeps its crontab line and is skipped when it fires. A scheduled run stops at the first question it is asked and keeps it for `pending` to show and `clear` to delete, and a firing that finds the previous one of the entry still running is skipped. An expression with a number outside its field's range, or a step of zero, is refused. ([#1546](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/1546), [#1541](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/1541))
@@ -36,6 +42,7 @@
  - Changed the refusal of `edit_file` on a file that is not vouched for, or that untrusted content was written to, to say the file is closed to `edit_file` and to name what is open: `write_file`, `run` and vouching for the path, each only where the session offers it. It no longer tells the agent to use a reference, which `edit_file` cannot take. ([#3688](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3688))
  - Changed the line that ends a turn the service refused as invalid (HTTP 400) to name the backend the request went to, the model you configured or chose, and the request fields the retries left out, such as the cache lifetime, the effort level or shown thinking, and to end with the next step: `/model`, or `bravebot doctor`. It shows no text from the service, and the desktop window's failure message is unchanged. ([#3713](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3713))
  - Changed a saved `model` of `xai/<name>` that a gateway lists as a bare name to select the xAI account, where before xAI sign-in it reached that gateway. `<gateway-id>/xai/<name>` still reaches the gateway. ([#3142](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3142))
+ - Fixed a check being made, and an empty release recorded, for a command that printed nothing when `run` was called with `read: true` or `read_output` was called on its reference. Neither makes a check, asks you anything or releases anything now. The result of `run` says the command printed nothing, `read_output` says the reference printed nothing, and the audit trail records that the output held no bytes. ([#3720](https://github.com/brave/bravebot/issues/3720))

